**user**:
> How can I add a "loading" graphic over a Leaflet map in Shiny while a new set of data is being loaded onto it?
**assistant**:
> To include a "loading" graphic while your Leaflet map in a Shiny application is getting updated with new data, you can leverage JavaScript and CSS along with Shiny's reactivity. Here's a step-by-step approach to achieve this:
> 
> 1. **Create a Loading Graphic**: You can use CSS to create a simple loading spinner. Add the CSS in the `www` directory or embedded within your UI.
> 
> 2. **Integrate JavaScript**: Use JavaScript to control the visibility of the loading spinner according to the data loading process.
> 
> 3. **Update the Shiny App**: Use Shiny reactive functions to toggle the loading spinner.
> 
> Here's a sample implementation:
> 
> ### 1. CSS for the Loading Spinner
> 
> Add the following CSS to your `www` directory in a file called `styles.css`, or directly in the `tags$head()` of your UI:
> 
> ```css
> #loading-spinner {
>   display: none;
>   position: fixed;
>   z-index: 1000; /* Ensure it is above everything */
>   top: 50%;
>   left: 50%;
>   margin-top: -30px; /* Half the height of the spinner */
>   margin-left: -30px; /* Half the width of the spinner */
> }
> 
> .spinner {
>   border: 4px solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
>   border-left: 4px solid #4285f4;
>   border-radius: 50%;
>   width: 60px;
>   height: 60px;
>   animation: spin 1s linear infinite;
> }
> 
> @keyframes spin {
>   to { transform: rotate(360deg); }
> }
> ```
> 
> ### 2. JavaScript for Showing/Hiding the Spinner
> 
> Include a bit of JavaScript in your UI (within `tags$head()`):
> 
> ```html
> <script type="text/javascript">
>   function showSpinner() {
>     document.getElementById('loading-spinner').style.display = 'block';
>   }
> 
>   function hideSpinner() {
>     document.getElementById('loading-spinner').style.display = 'none';
>   }
> 
>   Shiny.addCustomMessageHandler('show-loading', function(message) {
>     if (message === 'show') {
>       showSpinner();
>     } else if (message === 'hide') {
>       hideSpinner();
>     }
>   });
> </script>
> ```
> 
> ### 3. Modify Your Shiny Server and UI Code
> 
> In your `ui` function, include a placeholder for the spinner:
> 
> ```r
> library(shiny)
> 
> ui <- fluidPage(
>   tags$head(
>     tags$link(rel = "stylesheet", type = "text/css", href = "styles.css")
>   ),
>   div(id = "loading-spinner", class = "spinner"),
>   leafletOutput("mymap"),
>   actionButton("loadData", "Load New Data")
> )
> ```
> 
> In your `server` function, show and hide the spinner during the data loading process:
> 
> ```r
> server <- function(input, output, session) {
>   data <- reactiveVal(data.frame())
> 
>   observeEvent(input$loadData, {
>     session$sendCustomMessage(type = 'show-loading', message = 'show')
> 
>     # Simulate data loading with a delay
>     Sys.sleep(2) # replace with your actual data loading code
>     new_data <- data.frame(lat = runif(10, -90, 90), lng = runif(10, -180, 180))
>     data(new_data)
> 
>     session$sendCustomMessage(type = 'show-loading', message = 'hide')
>   })
> 
>   output$mymap <- renderLeaflet({
>     leaflet() %>%
>       addTiles() %>%
>       observe({
>         d <- data()
>         if(nrow(d) > 0) {
>           clearMarkers()
>           addMarkers(data = d, ~lng, ~lat)
>         }
>       })
>   })
> }
> ```
> 
> With this setup, the "loading" graphic will appear while the data is being processed and will disappear once the map has been updated. Adjust the simulated `Sys.sleep(2)` with the actual time taken by your data-loading process or remove it if it’s unnecessary in the real scenario.
